<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Email's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because almost all of
    its consumers are still in Base: nine Humans.Application services and six
    Humans.Infrastructure jobs build messages through IEmailMessageFactory and send them
    through IEmailService, Shell's ProfileController and UsersAdminController read a
    human's outbox through IEmailOutboxServiceRead, and ProcessEmailOutboxJob /
    CleanupEmailOutboxJob drive IEmailOutboxProcessor / IEmailOutboxRetention. A
    Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Email would therefore make Base reference a section
    and cycle (design §15.5b, memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    IImmediateOutboxProcessor is here for the opposite reason: Base *implements* it
    (HangfireImmediateOutboxProcessor enqueues the recurring job), and the section's
    OutboxEmailService consumes it.

    Everything with no consumer outside the section stays internal: the outbox admin
    surface (IEmailOutboxService), the renderer, the body composer, the SMTP transport,
    the repository and the entity.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, and EmailOutboxStatus which stayed
    there because three sections persist it), Humans.Events.Contracts (EventStatus, named by
    EventLifecycleNotification), and — new at G5 lane 3b (nobodies-collective/Humans#866) —
    Humans.Users.Contracts, for MembershipTier and MessageCategory.

    That third edge was NOT disclosed by the phase-3 recon, which predicted only Governance
    and Teams would gain one; it is the same mechanical consequence, found by the compiler.
    Both enums used to sit in Humans.Domain, which lane 3b emptied and deleted; both are
    Users' own vocabulary (MessageCategory is the communication-preference axis, MembershipTier
    the membership one) and both are named here by IEmailMessageFactory's audience parameters
    and EmailMessage's Category. This is the enums following their owner, not Email taking a
    dependency on Users' services.

    All three leaf-to-leaf edges are acyclic. Humans.Events.Contracts has zero
    ProjectReference; Humans.Users.Contracts has exactly one, to the equally
    zero-reference Humans.Onboarding.Contracts. Both chains terminate. Note that Base
    references Humans.Users.Contracts, so nothing in that project may ever reference this one.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Events.Contracts\Humans.Events.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Users.Contracts\Humans.Users.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
